ks around the around the  university and you know I would I had  the luxury of working with some  incredible people here one of whom was  Dean peizo this the Dean of the medical  school who was doing a lot of work to  make sure that the the work that was  done in the medical school would stand  up to any kind of scrutiny so that  relationships with companies were  carefully disclosed and managed properly  that's you know an example of  identifying a risk prioritizing the risk  and then mitigating the risk I love that  and you've worked across many different  sectors politics government business  academia are beyond strategy market are  beyond market strategies different from  sector to sector well I've had a pretty  varied career you're right the sectors  that I've worked in as you mentioned  politics and government and corporate  and and now academia there are some  commonalities and there are some  differences a lot of the public affairs  tried-and-true rules of the road seem to  transcend sectors and I think that's one  of the things that I've really enjoyed  about my career is that I've been able  to have kind of a bird's-eye view of all  of these sectors and try to identify  some of the things that are common to  all and then some of the differences you  know there's a big difference to what a  government  entity is facing compared to a  corporation and understanding those  differences are really important and how  do you feel that societal expectations  of companies have changed in regard to  non market issues well I mean all of us  as consumers certainly have a higher  standard for the kinds of products and  services that we avail ourselves of and  there are a lot more tools to compare  and contrast products and services  across a particular business category  but beyond that if take a look at  employees employees are a key  stakeholder group of any company right  you know  stakeholders of a company include  investors and consumers and customers  and and employees well employees have in  today's world are much more interested  in what does their company stand for and  so you're seeing employee pressure put  on company leadership just take stands  on issues that maybe twenty years ago a  company wouldn't feel that was an  appropriate thing to do so really  understanding your employee base and  whether or not you're going to get the  right employees or the best employees to  work for you you have to understand what  their needs wants and aspirations are  and stakeholder power seems to have  become even more intense how do you feel  this has happened well you look at how  stakeholders are able to mobilize today  and a lot of that has to do with new  media platforms the internet actually  provides a lot of tools for stakeholders  to communicate globally so that if  there's an issue that is of importance  to a certain stakeholder group they're  able to connect with others that have  the same interests as they have and by  doing that they can exert pressure on a  company they can mobilize  is both in a positive way and in a  negative way I think that's one of the  interests interesting things about the  Internet is that you know there's a kind  of a paradoxical nature of the Internet  that it it you know on the one hand kind  of democratizes information it provides  a platform so that so many people around  the world can understand things that  they might not have even confronted  before you take something like Darfur  the tragedy of Darfur in the Sudan you  know before the internet maybe people  would never have heard of that but  because of the internet that was a issue  that was brought to the public  consciousness and you know that was a  helpful thing by the same token the  internet also provides a platform for  bad actors and we've seen that play out  in a number of ways so you know when you  when you talk about how new technologies  intersect with stakeholders there is a  symbiotic relationship between the two  stakeholder power has been I think  enhanced by new technologies mm-hmm so  we're gonna switch gears a little bit  and talk about brand management so  everyone is talking about brand  management in the beyond market world  how do you feel that branding plays out  well I look I look at brand a little  differently than some in that the first  step that I go through in terms of  trying to understand you know brand as a  constant construct is to whom am I  making a promise I see brand as a  promise and all organizations have  multiple stakeholders you look at  Stanford Stanford has you know students  obviously faculty employees the  community the federal government we do a  billion dollars worth of federal  research here you would think that the  government wants us to do that well so  they are a stakeholder right donors now  to all of those stakeholders we make  either an explicit or an implicit  promise  so to a student it might be you know  you're gonna get a world-class education  to that students parent it might be  slightly different your son or daughter  is gonna get a world-class education but  in a safe environment because the safety  issues is of paramount concern to  parents that is not exactly the same  promise we might make to a donor a donor  wants to know that their dollars that  they provide to Stanford that those  dollars are going to be used wisely  that we're gonna be a good financial  steward so really digging down into what  are the promises that are made that all  roll up to what is the Stanford brand is  an important methodology I think to go  through now the interesting thing for me  because I've live in a world of  reputation management is what's the  relationship between brand and  reputation well I think there's a pretty  direct correlation that to those to whom  a promise has been made reputation is  about whether that promise has been kept  so brand is a promise  reputation is about keeping that promise  and companies that understand the value  of 