ave negative  impact than the story that is cauterized  immediately on the flip side and an  example of what's changed and I think  the worst first rule still generally  applies what's changed is because of  technology an old rule used to be don't  repeat the accusation if somebody calls  you a skunk don't say I deny that I am a  skunk well that was the old rule don't  say that you know you can describe why  you're not asked run skunk but you don't  use the word right well in today's world  the way people search for information  they're going to use the accusation  as their search tool so if you're if if  the information you put out on a on an  electronic platform doesn't contain  those search term that that that  phraseology they won't find your  response they won't find your  explanation and so you have to you have  to communicate on the platforms that  your adversaries are using and I think  that's the rule that if if people are if  you're seeing things on the net that are  troubling or damaging you've got to be  able to live in that world as well  interesting how do you see that values  play into crisis management well you  know if you think about this the speed  at which a crisis develops far faster  than it ever did before and so you may  not have all the information you would  like you may not know all the facts and  that might lead you to not respond you  know there's a natural tendency well we  don't have all the facts so we can't  really say anything well what you can  say is what your values are and what you  can talk about your values and that  whatever you do in response to a  particular crisis is going to be based  on the values of your organization or  your company companies that don't really  have clarity about their values and are  kind of coming up with them on the fly  they're at a tremendous disadvantage  conversely companies that have a very  clear sense of who they are what they  stand for even in the midst of crisis  they have competitive advantage so what  are some common features that you have  observed about how crises evolve and how  leaders have responded well it's a mixed  bag I think some leaders do a great job  managing crisis you know you go back to  the Johnson and Johnson famous case back  in the 80s  Jim Burke who was the chairman of the  board you know  with the tylenol case made a very  courageous decision that was based on  the values of his company and he got  great credit for that but had Johnson  and Johnson not known what its values  were he wouldn't have been able to make  the decisions that he made to take  tylenol off the shelves nationally and  do it in a way that was timely which is  also another issue okay interesting what  is the Iron Triangle and how does it  affect business strategies  well the Iron Triangle is really a term  of art that came to be about the  relationship between kind of the the  legislative branch in Washington the  bureaucracy on the executive side of the  coin and then interest groups and you  know it's generally not been perceived  as a very positive thing that there's  this sort of triangle of interest that  collude with each other to create a more  favorable environment for companies or  organizations that interact with it and  so it became a term of art probably  thirty years ago of this Iron Triangle  and now with more transparency you know  I think some of the issues that might  have existed years ago are you know a  little bit more in the light of day and  people can kind of understand a little  bit more of what forces are at work so  do you see any new paradigms for  managing stakeholder relationships well  I think that that if there's a paradigm  of that you know we could identify it is  how well do you understand your  stakeholders of your organization and  you know because remember people think  people don't do things for your reasons  they do things for their reasons and the  more you understand where they're coming  from the war you understand 